 "blocks": [
  {
   "type": "spec_point",
   "id": "spcpt_XT4rfZ58Z33Xs5pg",
   "name": "Basic Coordinate Geometry",
   "definition": null
  },
  {
   "type": "heading",
   "level": 2,
   "text": "Basic Coordinate Geometry"
  },
  {
   "type": "heading",
   "level": 3,
   "text": "What are Cartesian coordinates?"
  },
  {
   "type": "bullets",
   "md": "- **Cartesian** coordinates are the coordinates used in the *x-y *coordinate system\n\n  - They allow us to label where things are in a two-dimensional plane\n- In the 2D Cartesian system, the horizontal axis is labelled *x*  and the vertical axis is labelled *y*"
  },
  {
   "type": "heading",
   "level": 3,
   "text": "What can I do with coordinates?"
  },
  {
   "type": "bullets",
   "md": "- If you have two points with coordinates `open parentheses x subscript 1 comma space y subscript 1 close parentheses`* *and* *`open parentheses x subscript 2 comma space y subscript 2 close parentheses`* *then you should be able to find\n\n  - The **distance** between the two points\n  - The point that **divides** the line between the two points in a given **ratio**\n  - The **gradient** of the line joining the two points"
  },
  {
   "type": "heading",
   "level": 3,
   "text": "How do I find the distance between two points?"
  },
  {
   "type": "bullets",
   "md": "- The **distance **$d$ between two points with coordinates `open parentheses x subscript 1 comma space y subscript 1 close parentheses`* *and* *`open parentheses x subscript 2 comma space y subscript 2 close parentheses`* *can be found using the formula\n\n  - `d space equals blank square root of open parentheses x subscript 1 minus blank x subscript 2 close parentheses squared space plus space open parentheses y subscript 1 minus blank y subscript 2 close parentheses squared end root blank`"
  },
  {
   "type": "bullets",
   "md": "- $d$ is the **hypotenuse** of a right-angled triangle\n\n  - The differences between the $x$-coordinates and the $y$-coordinates give the other two side lengths\n  - So by Pythagoras’ Theorem  `d squared space equals space open parentheses x subscript 1 minus x subscript 2 close parentheses squared space plus space open parentheses y subscript 1 minus y subscript 2 close parentheses squared`"
  },
  {
   "type": "heading",
   "level": 3,
   "text": "How do I divide the line between two points in a given ratio?"
  },
  {
   "type": "bullets",
   "md": "- The coordinates of the point **dividing** the line joining `open parentheses x subscript 1 comma space y subscript 1 close parentheses` and `open parentheses x subscript 2 comma space y subscript 2 close parentheses` in the **ratio** $m:n$ are given by\n\n  - `open parentheses fraction numerator n x subscript 1 plus m x subscript 2 over denominator m plus n end fraction comma space fraction numerator n y subscript 1 plus m y subscript 2 over denominator m plus n end fraction close parentheses`"
  },
  {
   "type": "bullets",
   "md": "- If $m>n$ then the point is closer to `open parentheses x subscript 2 comma space y subscript 2 close parentheses`\n- If $m<n$ then the point is closer to `open parentheses x subscript 1 comma space y subscript 1 close parentheses`"
  },
  {
   "type": "heading",
   "level": 3,
   "text": "How do I find the midpoint between two points?"
  },
  {
   "type": "bullets",
   "md": "- The **midpoint** is the point **halfway between** the two points\n\n  - This is just 'dividing in a given ratio' where the ratio is $1:1$\n- The **coordinates** of the **midpoint** will be\n\n  - `open parentheses fraction numerator x subscript 1 plus blank x subscript 2 over denominator 2 end fraction blank comma blank fraction numerator y subscript 1 plus blank y subscript 2 over denominator 2 end fraction blank close parentheses`"
  },
  {
   "type": "figure",
   "orig_src": "https://cdn.savemyexams.com/uploads/2020/06/3.1.1-Basic-Coordinate-Geometry-Notes-Diagram-5.png",
   "file": "assets/0231e3ac2a05-3-1-1-basic-coordinate-geometry-notes-diagram-5.png",
   "alt": "Midpoint of a line segment",
   "caption": null
  },
  {
   "type": "heading",
   "level": 3,
   "text": "How do I find the gradient of a straight line joining two points?"
  },
  {
   "type": "bullets",
   "md": "- The **gradient** $m$ of a line between two points with coordinates `open parentheses x subscript 1 comma space y subscript 1 close parentheses`* *and* *`open parentheses x subscript 2 comma space y subscript 2 close parentheses`* *can be found using the formula\n\n  - $m=\\frac{y_{2}-y_{1}}{x_{2}-x_{1}}$"
  },
  {
   "type": "bullets",
   "md": "- This is also known as  $m=\\frac{\\mathrm{rise}}{\\mathrm{run}}$"
  },
  {
   "type": "callout",
   "variant": "worked-example",
   "label": "Worked Example",
   "md": "Point $A$ has coordinates `open parentheses 3 comma space minus 4 close parentheses` and point $B$ has coordinates `open parentheses negative 5 comma space 8 close parentheses`.\n\n(a) Calculate the distance between points $A$ and $B$.\n\n> *Let *`A open parentheses 3 comma space minus 4 close parentheses`*be 'point 1', so *$x_{1}=3$* and *$y_{1}=-4$\n\n*Let *`B open parentheses negative 5 comma space 8 close parentheses` *be 'point 2', so *$x_{2}=-5$* and *$y_{2}=8$\n\n> *Use the formula  *`d space equals blank square root of open parentheses x subscript 1 minus blank x subscript 2 close parentheses squared space plus space open parentheses y subscript 1 minus blank y subscript 2 close parentheses squared end root blank`\n\n`d space equals space square root of open parentheses 3 minus open parentheses negative 5 close parentheses close parentheses squared space plus space open parentheses negative 4 minus 8 close parentheses squared end root space equals blank square root of 8 squared plus open parentheses negative 12 close parentheses squared end root space equals space square root of 64 plus 144 end root`\n\n**Final answer:** $\\sqrt{208}$**  ****(or **$4\\sqrt{13}$** ****or 14.4 to 3 s.f.)**\n\n(b) Find the gradient of the line joining points *A *and *B*.\n\n> *Let *`A open parentheses 3 comma space minus 4 close parentheses`* be 'point 1', so *$x_{1}=3$* and *$y_{1}=-4$\n\n*Let *`B open parentheses negative 5 comma space 8 close parentheses` *be 'point 2', so *$x_{2}=-5$* and *$y_{2}=8$\n\n> *Use the formula *$m=\\frac{y_{2}-y_{1}}{x_{2}-x_{1}}$\n\n`m equals fraction numerator 8 minus open parentheses negative 4 close parentheses over denominator negative 5 minus 3 end fraction equals fraction numerator 12 over denominator negative 8 end fraction`\n\n> *Simplify the fraction*\n\n$-\\frac{3}{2}$\n\n(c) Find point that divides the line joining points *A* and *B* in the ratio $1:3$.\n\n> *Let *`A open parentheses 3 comma space minus 4 close parentheses`*